          "example": "<p>“Make these notes useful” gives the reader little guidance. Try four ingredients:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Context:</strong> only the source facts needed for this task.</li>\n<li><strong>Task:</strong> what to produce.</li>\n<li><strong>Shape and limits:</strong> how the result should be organised and what must stay unknown.</li>\n<li><strong>Check:</strong> how you will judge it before using it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>You do not need a magic phrase or a long prompt. These ingredients make your intention inspectable. They cannot guarantee that an answer will follow it.</p>\n<h4>Technical worked request</h4>\n<p><strong>Source T2:</strong> The sign-up form accepts email and password. It must explain invalid input without exposing whether an email already has an account. No tests have been run.</p>\n<blockquote><p>Propose test cases for invalid input and account privacy. Use columns for sample input, expected behavior, requirement checked and execution status. Use invented addresses. Record every execution status as “not run”; do not report observed results. I will compare coverage with the requirements and run the tests separately.</p></blockquote>\n<p>This gives a reviewer something to evaluate without suggesting that a test plan proves the form works.</p>",
