          "transfer": "<p>Classify the two claims in your route, then rewrite them without an unsupported assertion.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Fresh source</th><th>Claims to check</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>L3-E-X</code></td><td>A reading group has nine people and meets for forty-five minutes on Wednesday. A room has not been chosen.</td><td>“The group has twelve people.” “The group meets in Room 4.”</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>",
          "comparison": "<details><summary>Compare your reasoning</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Everyday:</strong> Twelve conflicts with nine; Room 4 is not given. Repair: “Nine people will meet for forty-five minutes on Wednesday; the room is to be confirmed.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If your repair simply deletes everything, restore the facts the source does support. A useful check preserves information as well as removing mistakes.</p>\n</details>",
          "own": "<p>Use an invented answer to your Lesson 2 request, or deliberately introduce one changed fact and one missing detail into a sample above. No external tool call is necessary.</p>\n<p>Create a small table: <strong>claim / source evidence / status / correction or next check</strong>. Then write the corrected answer and finish: “I changed … because ….”</p>\n<p><strong>Self-check rubric:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Observable evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Separate claims</td><td>A mixed sentence is split where facts could have different statuses.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Classify accurately</td><td>Each supported/contradicted claim has source evidence; each not-given claim names what is missing.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Repair usefully</td><td>Correct facts remain; incompatible facts change; unknown facts stay visibly unknown.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Explain a revision</td><td>You connect at least one change to source evidence rather than “it sounds better.”</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Mark each criterion <strong>shown</strong> or <strong>needs another try</strong> and keep the explanation. This is your self-check, not a certificate or an automatic grade.</p>\n<p><strong>Take forward:</strong> your source, request, claim table and corrected draft are one small learning artifact. In <a href=\"#lesson=4\">Lesson 4: Choose what to use and share</a>, decide which information to use and who may see it before asking another person for feedback.</p>"
